window.docContents[227].resolve({"/Related-Work/#related-work":{"contents":"Computing a PDE residual, bounding an approximation error, and constructing a\nsolution are different mathematical tasks. A symbolic identity can verify a\ncandidate formula; a numerical enclosure can certify an estimate; an existence\nproof must supply the analytic argument connecting its hypotheses to a solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"2. Related Work","id":"/Related-Work/#related-work"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Hodograph-and-Local-Legendre-Transformations--Using-the-unknown-as-a-coordinate--A-potential-for-the-inverse-velocity":{"contents":"Let  be a prescribed function of velocity.\nThe compressible-flow system used here is\n\n\n\nSubstituting the inverse matrix into the first equation and dividing by\nthe nonzero signed determinant gives\n\n\n\nThis equation is linear in . The transformed curl equation says that\n is symmetric. On a ball inside the target, that symmetry supplies a\npotential  with . The ball matters: local inversion\nalone does not give a global potential on an arbitrary target domain.\n\nSuppose  is open, ,\n, and .\nLet  be arbitrary, and suppose \nsatisfies both displayed compressible-flow equations throughout .\nThere are a local inverse chart , with\n, and  such that\n. On this ball there is a  function \nwith  andHere .\n\nConstruct the regular inverse chart. The cofactor identities give the\nlinear mass equation and preserve zero curl, with the signed determinant.\nChoose a positive-radius ball around  inside the open target.\nThe  inverse field has symmetric derivative on this convex ball,\nso the potential theorem constructs  with .\nSubstitution and symmetry of the second derivatives give the scalar equation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Hodograph and Local Legendre Transformations\u0009Using the unknown as a coordinate","header":"9.2.1.6.1.2. A potential for the inverse velocity","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Hodograph-and-Local-Legendre-Transformations--Using-the-unknown-as-a-coordinate--A-potential-for-the-inverse-velocity"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces":{"contents":"Hölder spaces record a quantitative form of continuity:\n is controlled by a power . They are the natural\ntarget of Morrey's supercritical Sobolev embeddings and the regularity class\nin which many compactness and nonlinear arguments recover pointwise control.\n\nFor example, on ,\n\n\n\nThus  is Hölder with exponent  and constant one.\nIt is not Lipschitz there, because\n is unbounded as .\nThe exponent describes how quickly oscillation shrinks with distance; it\ndoes not assert that a classical derivative exists or is bounded. A Hölder\nseminorm also ignores additive constants, which is why the bounded Hölder\nnorm includes the size of the function itself.\n\nThree layers must be kept separate. Mathlib's HolderWith C γ f is a\nquantitative predicate with a specified constant ; MemHolder γ f says\nthat some finite constant works; and HolderSpace.C0GammaOn Ω γ is the\ncompleted normed space used as a target of Sobolev embeddings. The first two\nlayers and the least-constant construction come from Mathlib; PDEAtlas adds\nonly the completed space of bounded real-valued Hölder functions.\n\nThe domain parameter  is an arbitrary metric type. For a geometric\nset , one takes the subtype with its inherited metric; no\nopenness, measurability, Euclidean structure, or boundedness of the set is\nneeded to define the space. The functions themselves are required to be\nbounded. Thus, on an unbounded domain this is the bounded Hölder space, not the\nspace of all Hölder functions.\n\nUnlike a Sobolev graph space, this type stores an actual pointwise\nrepresentative. Equality is pointwise, evaluation is meaningful at every\npoint, and restriction is literal function restriction. A later Morrey\nembedding therefore has two obligations: construct an element of this\nrepresentative space and prove that it agrees almost everywhere with the\noriginal Sobolev coordinate.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings","header":"4.3.3. Hölder Spaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces"}});